{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '270,345p'\nnl -ba test/dao/space/query_test.cljc | sed -n '320,430p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '471,505p;638,660p;680,706p'\nrg -n \\\"snapshot\\\" collab/architect-space-index-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | head -30\"","aggregated_output":"   270\t\n   271\t;; =============================================================================\n   272\t;; snapshot: the one dao.stream interpreter (Decision 3)\n   273\t;; =============================================================================\n   274\t\n   275\t(defn snapshot\n   276\t  \"Turn a dao.stream reader handle into a relation value:\n   277\t\n   278\t     {:relation <relation value>\n   279\t      :status   :ended | :blocked | :gap | :defect\n   280\t      :cursor   c            ; the cursor reached; :gap keeps the last retained\n   281\t      :recovery c'}          ; :gap only\n   282\t      :read     raw}         ; :defect only\n   283\t\n   284\t   Mints at :dao.stream/oldest and loops observe/step with a total effect\n   285\t   (conj onto the accumulator, answering ok) until the step stops. Every\n   286\t   stopping outcome is data: :blocked is an open stream caught up (a\n   287\t   snapshot at call time), :gap carries the values read before the hole and\n   288\t   the recovery cursor, :defect the raw answer. The caller decides whether a\n   289\t   partial snapshot is a usable relation. The handle is never closed, and a\n   290\t   cursor never advances past a value the accumulator did not retain (the\n   291\t   step's effect-before-commit).\"\n   292\t  [handle]\n   293\t  (let [mint (stream/cursor handle :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   294\t    (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome mint))\n   295\t      {:relation (relation [])\n   296\t       :status :defect\n   297\t       :cursor nil\n   298\t       :read mint}\n   299\t      (loop [cursor (:dao.stream/cursor mint), values []]\n   300\t        (let [acc (volatile! values)\n   301\t              step (observe/step handle cursor\n   302\t                                 (fn [value]\n   303\t                                   (vswap! acc conj value)\n   304\t                                   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))]\n   305\t          (case (:status step)\n   306\t            :advance (recur (:cursor step) @acc)\n   307\t            :retry {:relation (relation @acc)\n   308\t                    :status :blocked\n   309\t                    :cursor cursor}\n   310\t            :ended {:relation (relation @acc)\n   311\t                    :status :ended\n   312\t                    :cursor cursor}\n   313\t            :gap {:relation (relation @acc)\n   314\t                  :status :gap\n   315\t                  :cursor cursor\n   316\t                  :recovery (:recovery step)}\n   317\t            ;; :defect, and :failed (impossible for this total effect,\n   318\t            ;; classified the same way if a composition ever produces it)\n   319\t            {:relation (relation @acc)\n   320\t             :status :defect\n   321\t             :cursor cursor\n   322\t             :read (:read step)}))))))\n   323\t\n   324\t\n   325\t;; =============================================================================\n   326\t;; Realize a db-value: value tag -> {::relation ::fact-index ::indexes}\n   327\t;; =============================================================================\n   328\t\n   329\t(defn- relation->fact-index\n   330\t  [relation]\n   331\t  (index/index-datoms (mapv (fn [tuple]\n   332\t                              [(nth tuple 0) (nth tuple 1)\n   333\t                               (nth tuple 2) 0 datom/default-op])\n   334\t                            relation)))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t(defn- force-relation\n   338\t  \"Force a relation if it is delayed (e.g. from a lazy covered-index\n   339\t   realization) or return it directly. Memoization belongs to the delay:\n   340\t   forcing is therefore at most one walk, but this function itself neither\n   341\t   creates nor guarantees non-nilness.\"\n   342\t  [relation]\n   343\t  (if (delay? relation) @relation relation))\n   344\t\n   345\t\n   320\t\n   321\t\n   322\t(deftest results-are-distinct\n   323\t  (let [datoms [[1 :kind :cat 1 1] [1 :pet true 1 1]]]\n   324\t    (is (= #{[1]}\n   325\t           (qcur '[:find ?e :where (or [?e :kind :cat] [?e :pet true])]\n   326\t                 datoms)))))\n   327\t\n   328\t\n   329\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   330\t;; O: ownership — the caller owns every handle (proved with the published\n   331\t;; helpers further down; deftest bodies resolve at run time)\n   332\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   333\t\n   334\t\n   335\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   336\t;; S: snapshot — the one dao.stream interpreter\n   337\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   338\t\n   339\t(deftest snapshot-of-an-open-buffer-is-blocked\n   340\t  (let [h (ring-handle 4096)]\n   341\t    (doseq [v [:a :b :c]] (stream/append! h v))\n   342\t    (let [snap (query/snapshot h)]\n   343\t      (is (= :blocked (:status snap)) \"an open stream caught up\")\n   344\t      (is (= [:a :b :c] (:dao.space.query/relation (:relation snap))))\n   345\t      (is (= :dao.stream/blocked\n   346\t             (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next h (:cursor snap))))\n   347\t          \"the snapshot's cursor sits after the third value\"))))\n   348\t\n   349\t\n   350\t(deftest snapshot-of-a-closed-buffer-ends\n   351\t  (let [h (ring-handle 4096)]\n   352\t    (doseq [v [:a :b]] (stream/append! h v))\n   353\t    (stream/close! h)\n   354\t    (let [snap (query/snapshot h)]\n   355\t      (is (= :ended (:status snap)))\n   356\t      (is (= [:a :b] (:dao.space.query/relation (:relation snap)))))))\n   357\t\n   358\t\n   359\t(deftest snapshot-of-a-gap-is-data\n   360\t  ;; A snapshot mints at :dao.stream/oldest, which by construction never\n   361\t  ;; trails a quiescent ringbuffer's first retained position, so a real\n   362\t  ;; values-then-hole gap is pinned with a scripted reader: :a is read and\n   363\t  ;; retained, then the position is evicted.\n   364\t  (let [h (reify stream/IDaoStreamReader\n   365\t            (cursor\n   366\t              [_ _]\n   367\t              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   368\t               :dao.stream/cursor {::pos 0}})\n   369\t\n   370\t            (next\n   371\t              [_ c]\n   372\t              (case (::pos c)\n   373\t                0 {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   374\t                   :dao.stream/value :a\n   375\t                   :dao.stream/cursor {::pos 1}}\n   376\t                1 {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/gap\n   377\t                   :dao.stream/cursor {::pos 2}})))\n   378\t        snap (query/snapshot h)]\n   379\t    (is (= :gap (:status snap)))\n   380\t    (is (= [:a] (:dao.space.query/relation (:relation snap)))\n   381\t        \"the values read before the hole are retained\")\n   382\t    (is (= {::pos 1} (:cursor snap)) \"the last retained position\")\n   383\t    (is (= {::pos 2} (:recovery snap)) \"the recovery cursor\")))\n   384\t\n   385\t\n   386\t(deftest snapshot-of-a-defect-carries-the-raw-answer\n   387\t  (let [h (reify stream/IDaoStreamReader\n   388\t            (cursor\n   389\t              [_ _]\n   390\t              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   391\t               :dao.stream/cursor {::pos 0}})\n   392\t\n   393\t            (next\n   394\t              [_ _]\n   395\t              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch}))\n   396\t        snap (query/snapshot h)]\n   397\t    (is (= :defect (:status snap)))\n   398\t    (is (= :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n   399\t           (:dao.stream/outcome (:read snap))))))\n   400\t\n   401\t\n   402\t(deftest snapshot-never-closes-the-handle\n   403\t  (let [h (ring-handle 4096)]\n   404\t    (stream/append! h :a)\n   405\t    (query/snapshot h)\n   406\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! h :b)))\n   407\t        \"the handle still accepts appends after a snapshot\")))\n   408\t\n   409\t\n   410\t(deftest snapshot-relation-feeds-current-and-q\n   411\t  (let [h (ring-handle 4096)]\n   412\t    (doseq [d [[1 :sensor/x 1.0 0 1] [2 :sensor/x 2.0 0 1]]]\n   413\t      (stream/append! h d))\n   414\t    (let [snap (query/snapshot h)]\n   415\t      (is (= [[1 :sensor/x 1.0] [2 :sensor/x 2.0]]\n   416\t             (query/rows (query/current (:relation snap)))))\n   417\t      (is (= #{[1.0] [2.0]}\n   418\t             (query/collect\n   419\t               (query/q '[:find ?x :where [_ :sensor/x ?x]]\n   420\t                        (query/current (:relation snap)))))))))\n   421\t\n   422\t\n   423\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   424\t;; E: the evaluator surface (match / pull / :in / negation / aggregation)\n   425\t;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   426\t\n   427\t(deftest generic-relation-tuples-carry-arbitrary-dimensions\n   428\t  (let [tuples [[42] [1 :edge/to 2] [7 :sensor/x 1.0 2.0]]]\n   429\t    (is (= #{[42]} (qq '[:find ?v :where [?v]] (rel tuples))))\n   430\t    (is (= #{[1 2]}\n   471\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   472\t\n   473\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   474\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   475\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   476\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   477\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   478\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   479\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   480\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   481\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   482\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   483\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   484\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   485\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   486\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   487\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   488\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   489\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   490\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   491\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   492\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   493\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   494\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   495\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   496\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   497\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   498\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   499\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   500\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   501\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   502\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   503\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   504\t  descriptor envelope.\n   505\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   638\tthe oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send\n   639\tbuffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —\n   640\t`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or\n   641\tpermanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the\n   642\toutcomes and standardizes no policy.\n   643\t\n   644\t**Eviction never waits on a reader.** Retention is the stream's own declared\n   645\tpolicy and applies whatever any reader is doing — a stream holds no reader\n   646\tpositions, so it cannot be held back by a slow one. A transport that deferred\n   647\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   648\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   649\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   650\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a reader that falls behind is\n   651\ttold what it missed.\n   652\t\n   653\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   654\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   655\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   656\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   657\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it\n   658\tmissed values.\n   659\t\n   660\t## Composition\n   680\t## Explicitly Absent\n   681\t\n   682\tAbsent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants:\n   683\t\n   684\t- **an ambient registry** — a namespace-global dispatch table, or registration\n   685\t  as a load-time side effect. Host dispatch is ordinary data the host owns.\n   686\t- **a `closed?` predicate** — a predicate answer is stale the moment it\n   687\t  returns; operation results are authoritative.\n   688\t- **destructive reads** — a read that removes what it observes makes one\n   689\t  reader's progress every other reader's data loss. It is coherent only while\n   690\t  exactly one observer exists; a forwarder, an indexer, or a DHT replica makes\n   691\t  it theft. Where the capability went, so no later plan reinvents it: **taking\n   692\t  is a write.** Removing a tuple from an append-only log is not a mutation but\n   693\t  an assertion that it no longer holds, which `dao.space` already expresses as\n   694\t  a retraction datom — Linda's `in` is observe-then-retract, not a stream\n   695\t  operation. And the hard part of a take was never the removal but the\n   696\t  *exclusion* of competing takers, which is a lease over datoms\n   697\t  (`dao.lease.md`), not something a stream can promise. v1's `drain-one!`\n   698\t  appeared to solve exclusion only because it ran in one process against a\n   699\t  local ring buffer.\n   700\t- **`seek`** — every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and\n   701\t  kept cursors cover repositioning.\n   702\t- **a blocking take** — no operation waits.\n   703\t- **waiter registration** — there is no readiness extension.\n   704\t- **throwing conveniences** — operational outcomes are data.\n   705\t\n   706\t## The v2 namespace is transient\n62:The blocker is not `snapshot-datoms`; it is `publish-index!`'s signature.\n75:  local-stream handling to v2 under its own plan; index's snapshot becomes a\n83:drain to the transactor. It would remove index's *snapshot* v1 dependency now,\n90:`dao.space.query.md` records that `snapshot` moves to `dao.stream.observe`\n91:as `drain` when a second consumer appears. It has not. `snapshot-datoms` reads\n99:The second consumer is index's snapshot **after** D1's ordering completes.\n104:  functions do not actually disagree today: `query/snapshot` returns\n106:  query input; `snapshot-datoms` throws on a gap because `publish-index!`\n109:  total step. `drain` returns the status; `snapshot-datoms` becomes\n111:  (throw …)))` and `query/snapshot` becomes a thin `drain` caller. Neither\n113:- **`query/snapshot` is `drain` plus a relation tag; `snapshot-datoms` is\n157:Scope: the snapshot surface (restructured here) and the published surface\n160:**The snapshot**\n167:| S4 | `:blocked` and `:end` finish the snapshot at the current tail | `[D]` |\n171:| S8 | Flattening happens incrementally during the drain | `[T✗]` — an accident of the loop's shape. Nothing can observe it: no emission occurs until the snapshot completes. Dropped so the drain and the vocabulary can separate. |\n206:      This is the vocabulary half of the snapshot. The reading half lives in\n207:      `snapshot-datoms` and is the namespace's last dao.stream v1 dependency.\"\n212:3. Rewrite `snapshot-datoms` as drain-then-flatten: accumulate `(:ok result)`\n230:   transaction record\" message. Every existing snapshot test stays as written\n242:1. *What the library owns* → **The snapshot** bullet: split it into the\n244:   (`snapshot-datoms` — S1, S4), and state that the reading is v1.\n251:   | `snapshot-datoms`' `ds/next` loop (index.cljc:451-468) and `publish-index!`'s v1 `local-stream` input | `dao.space.transactor`'s plan | Migrate the local stream and the `:transactor` wrapper to v2; then `snapshot-datoms` = `observe/drain` + `datoms-from-elements`, and this is the second `drain` consumer, so promote `query/snapshot`'s loop to `dao.stream.observe/drain` in the same move (D2). `dao.space.schema`'s two `index/snapshot-datoms` call sites (926, 969) follow. |\n259:**`docs/design/dao.space.query.md`** — *Snapshots*: replace \"`snapshot` stays\n261:split from D2 (the second consumer is `index/snapshot-datoms` once the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
