gn/adr/0001-dao-space-as-storage-boundary.md:43:| **Transactor (write)** | **Decentralized** — every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own transactor. No central writer, no global commit. |\ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:39:- **No new mechanism is required, on either side.** `dao.space.transactor/DaoStreamLog`\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:191:`{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s :intake-pool [...] optional :name}`; it\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:202:(def log (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:77:  `dao.stream` is its own transactor: it writes atomic transaction records to its local\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:476:`(not [_ :work/claims ?w])` clause executes as written; `{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:477::intake-pool [...]}` is a registered `dao.stream` type (`dao.space.transactor`) whose\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:509:  (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:65:{:dao.stream/type :dao.space.index/published\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:6:- **[`dao.space.index`](dao.space.index.md)** — the transactor side: each agent builds\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:59:- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the transactor-side indexing library: every agent indexes its own datoms; the covered-index realization both sides share\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:77:  `dao.stream` is its own transactor: it writes atomic transaction records to its local\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:78:  stream, with no global contention and no commit step. It also carries the transactor's\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:96:all three.** The transactor writes and indexes; storage persists; the query matches. The\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:170:  (index/published-index\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:222:convention — is owned by [`dao.space.index`](dao.space.index.md), the transactor-side\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:232:transactor-side indexing library, `docs/design/dao.space.index.md`) snapshots the agent's\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:384:The write path does not end at the append. In Datomic the transactor also builds the covered\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:387:but without a central transactor process:\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:390:   records (see `dao.space.transactor`). The local stream is the durable record; no\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:406:agent-transactor loop*.\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:476:`(not [_ :work/claims ?w])` clause executes as written; `{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:477::intake-pool [...]}` is a registered `dao.stream` type (`dao.space.transactor`) whose\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:493:         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:509:  (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:517:    (let [{:keys [manifest-address]} (transactor/publish! log)]  ; enqueue the indexes\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:519:      (index/published-index store-coordinate manifest-address)))) ; read coordinate\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:539:appends, and both then claim the same task. That is not a storage bug, and not a transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:550:own — each claim's `t` is a per-local-stream transaction counter (`dao.space.transactor`\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:558:guarantee `dao.jing` or `dao.space.transactor` enforces — which is exactly why the\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:16:- `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the transactor-side realization this\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:57:(index/published-index\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:201:  `index/published-index`, opens the declared DaoJing content store, validates\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:244:  machinery; amortized reads. Still no transactor and no global clock — each\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.md:252:  transactor-built index. Implemented on JVM,\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:24:In Datomic, the **transactor** builds the covered indexes and saves them to\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:26:the transactor: every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:27:transactor (`dao.space.md`, *Three Boundaries*) — so every agent also owns the\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:28:transactor's other duty, **indexing its own datoms**. That duty needs a\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:40:sides share.** It is the same move Datomic makes between transactor and peer —\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:41:one party materializes, many parties consume — except here \"the transactor\"\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:67:- **The snapshot** — `snapshot-datoms` reads an agent-local stream from\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:86:- **The transportable read coordinate** — `published-index` constructs a\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:93:- **The transactor entry point** — `publish-index!`: snapshot the stream,\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:126:;; the transactor's move (JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart)\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:137:(index/snapshot-datoms local-stream)              ; => flattened datom seq\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:140:(index/published-index {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file :path path}\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:187:## The agent-transactor loop\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:189:The write path runs through `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:191:`{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s :intake-pool [...] optional :name}`; it\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:197:         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:202:(def log (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:209:(transactor/publish! log)                           ; 2. snapshot, build, enqueue\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:230:- The published-index DaoStream adapter opens lazily (manifest fetch plus\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:240:dao.space.transactor  ──►  dao.space.index  ◄──  dao.space.query\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:257:- `dao.space.transactor` requires `dao.space.index` for snapshotting,\ndocs/design/dao.space.index.md:296:  transactors: today an owner republishes wholesale from the full datom seq. A\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:64:schema is an expression of a centralized transactor's authority, and dao.space\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:66:transactor.\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:113:enforcer with authority over every writer — a central transactor,\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:127:The interpreter is two clients, one per boundary: the **schema transactor**\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:246:Datomic's schema is enforced by its transactor. Here the schema interpreter\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:247:is a library duty wrapping `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:291:`(schema/transactor local-stream intake-pool {:strict true})`. The default\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:340:  minting — the transactor's own ruling forbids it: \":db/id is required: a\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:342:  appends\" (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc`, `entity->datoms`). New\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:364:current-state index, built from a bounded snapshot after the inner transactor\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:374:wrapper, mirroring the inner transactor's single-writer boundary; no check can\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:382:reopen is required. Ownership is explicit: `schema/transactor`\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:383:opens the inner `:transactor` and owns it — its `close!` delegates inward,\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:421:1. **Explicit causality** is a project invariant, and the transactor already\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:552:`published-index` realization — it cannot reuse that opener directly, since\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:569:| The agent itself | The validating wrapper, opt-in, rejecting only in strict mode — every agent is its own transactor, so schema binds exactly those who adopt it, as tightly as they declare |\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:602:`dao.space.query` and `dao.space.transactor`; neither ever requires it. The\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:613:(def log (schema/transactor local-stream intake-pool))\ndocs/design/dao.space.schema.md:683:  `:count` (the §5 deferral stands); the transactor preserves explicit `m`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '220,265p'\nnl -ba docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md | sed -n '1,70p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '60,110p;180,240p;250,310p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '235,255p;355,390p;540,560p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '220,265p'\nnl -ba docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md | sed -n '1,70p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.md | sed -n '60,110p;180,240p;250,310p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '235,255p;355,390p;540,560p'\"","aggregated_output":"   220\t  `t` and `:dao/agent`, and every reader applies the lease and winner rules as the same\n   221\t  documented queries (a `not-join` with a `[(< ?now ?exp)]` predicate for availability; a\n   222\t  claims query binding the datom `t` for the `[t agent]` tie-break). Exercised by the\n   223\t  `claim-leases` test.\n   224\t- **Randomized backoff before claiming** (*still open*). Each worker waits a small random\n   225\t  interval before depositing a claim, decorrelating claim times from model latency and\n   226\t  spreading work across the fleet. Cheap, prompt-level, and composable with leases.\n   227\t\n   228\tNeither needs library or storage changes: leases are a writer convention plus a read-side\n   229\tquery rule, exactly as predicted.\n   230\t\n   231\t## The minimum viable stack\n   232\t\n   233\tFor a working multi-agent system on today's code, trusted agents only — no coordinator, no\n   234\tdeposit API, no new namespaces:\n   235\t\n   236\t1. **One `dao.jing.file` content handle**, made network-accessible with\n   237\t   `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers`. A remote reader uses\n   238\t   `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`; both handles expose the same plain-data content\n   239\t   effects.\n   240\t2. **Writes**: each agent owns a local `dao.stream` and opens one `:transactor` with that\n   241\t   stream plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool. It commits entity maps or datom vectors\n   242\t   through `ds/append!`/`transact!`, using integer stream-local entity ids; the wrapper\n   243\t   assigns transaction `t`. `publish!` appends covered-index blobs and a manifest to one\n   244\t   selected intake stream.\n   245\t3. **Materialization and reads**: a DaoJing observer consumes the supplied intake pool and\n   246\t   materializes its opaque payloads into `dao.jing`. Readers query exactly the\n   247\t   inputs they care about: immutable DaoStream descriptor db-values that may\n   248\t   resolve content locally or remotely. \"Available work\" is one query (negation + lease predicate);\n   249\t   claims and results are joins; every reader applies the documented winner rule.\n   250\t4. A pinned attribute vocabulary and these conventions in every agent's system prompt\n   251\t   (gap 3, manual version).\n   252\t\n   253\tThe contract is executable: `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj` runs the full loop —\n   254\tpost, associative discovery, racing claims, leases, retraction, settle — over the wire,\n   255\tand leaves the space on disk for post-hoc inspection with `dao.space.query`.\n   256\t\n   257\tEverything else — discovery, indexed snapshots, controlled mode — makes\n   258\tthe system better without changing what the agents already do: read the medium, decide,\n   259\tdeposit a trace.\n     1\t# ADR 0003: dao.space Is the Event Medium; There Is No System Event Bus\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status:** Accepted (2026-08-29)\n     4\t\n     5\t**Relates to:** `datom.world.md` invariant 3 (every callback is events on a stream) and its\n     6\tHost Boundaries section; `dao.space.md` § Coordination: Stigmergy.\n     7\t\n     8\t## Context\n     9\t\n    10\tMaking invariant 3 conform requires a host boundary to deposit its events somewhere. An\n    11\tadapter is a map of functions that transform host events to data on a stream\n    12\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries), which leaves open what stream.\n    13\t\n    14\tThe first answer proposed was one private ingress stream per transport endpoint, with its\n    15\tinterpreter reading that stream. It is the obvious answer and it is wrong, for a reason worth\n    16\trecording: an interpreter would have to be handed the endpoint's stream, so something must\n    17\tknow both the producer and the consumer and connect them. That is the coupling invariant 3\n    18\texists to remove, moved from a function call to a stream reference and declared solved.\n    19\t\n    20\tCorrecting that raises the real question. If interpreters must find traces without knowing\n    21\ttheir source, the traces need a shared medium — and the obvious name for it is a system event\n    22\tbus.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Decision\n    25\t\n    26\t**There is no system event bus. `dao.space` is the medium.**\n    27\t\n    28\tdatom.world is a tuple space because the tuple space is the formal model of stigmergy; the\n    29\tlineage is blackboard → Linda → `dao.space` (`dao.space.md` § Lineage). A system event bus\n    30\twould therefore be a *second* stigmergic medium standing beside the one the project exists to\n    31\tbe, with its own retention, its own addressing, and its own set of interpreters — duplicating\n    32\tthe blackboard rather than using it.\n    33\t\n    34\tHost boundaries deposit into `dao.space`. Interpreters read it, and deposit their derived\n    35\tfacts back into it at a higher level of abstraction.\n    36\t\n    37\t## Consequences\n    38\t\n    39\t- **No new mechanism is required, on either side.** `dao.space.transactor/DaoStreamLog`\n    40\t  already satisfies `dao.stream`'s `IDaoStreamWriter`, and its `-append!` accepts an entity\n    41\t  map or datom vector and expands it to datoms. An adapter transform returning an ordinary\n    42\t  map is already a legitimate deposit.\n    43\t\n    44\t- **The layering stays one-way, and this is why.** `dao.space` requires `dao.stream`;\n    45\t  `dao.stream` requires nothing from `dao.space`. A boundary that *named* `dao.space` would\n    46\t  invert that. It does not need to: an adapter is handed a stream and cannot tell what is\n    47\t  behind it, so the composition happens above, where something already knows about both\n    48\t  layers. The generality of the adapter definition is what preserves the layering.\n    49\t\n    50\t- **Volume and durability are retention questions, not admission questions.** Data is syntax\n    51\t  (axiom 2), so no depositing layer may filter by presumed worth. The operational answer\n    52\t  lives with the selected medium's transport, which declares its capacity and retention\n    53\t  policy (evict-oldest versus reject); `dao.stream` standardizes only the outcomes —\n    54\t  `:dao.stream/full`, `:dao.stream/gap` — so loss is never silent and a reader is never\n    55\t  silently repositioned.\n    56\t\n    57\t- **The medium must answer with data.** An adapter entry runs on a host callback thread, where\n    58\t  a thrown exception has nowhere to go, and `dao.stream`'s axiom is that all expected\n    59\t  operational outcomes are data. `dao.space.transactor`'s writer face does not yet satisfy\n    60\t  this: `-append!` throws on a closed wrapper, on invalid input, and on a refused inner\n    61\t  append — including `:dao.stream/full`, which is ordinary backpressure. Fixing that writer\n    62\t  face is a precondition of depositing through it.\n    63\t\n    64\t- **A rejected alternative, recorded because it recurs.** Splitting deposits by kind — events\n    65\t  to the medium, high-volume payload kept private — is the same error as the private ingress,\n    66\t  in different clothes: a depositing layer deciding which data deserves the medium. The\n    67\t  distinction that does survive is addressing, not worth: the contents of a channel resolved\n    68\t  by name are asked for, not discovered.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Open Questions\n    60\t  local d5 datoms only. Source scope belongs to the query interpreter and is\n    61\t  not appended to an indexed tuple.\n    62\t- **The in-memory index value** — `index-datoms` builds\n    63\t  `{:eavt :aevt :avet :vaet}` `dao.data.btree` sorted sets. `subseq-from`\n    64\t  delegates to `dao.data.btree/slice`: a log-n descent that, on a restored\n    65\t  tree, loads only the seek path plus the matching range. The implementation\n    66\t  is the same on JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n    67\t- **The snapshot** — `snapshot-datoms` reads an agent-local stream from\n    68\t  cursor zero with `ds/next`. A stream element is either one canonical datom\n    69\t  vector `[e a v t m]` or one atomic transaction record\n    70\t  `{:dao.space/transaction {:t n :datoms [...]}}`; transaction records are\n    71\t  validated and flattened into their datoms. `:blocked` and `:end` finish the\n    72\t  snapshot at the current tail; `:daostream/gap` and malformed stream results,\n    73\t  datoms, or transaction records throw. Because the snapshot starts at\n    74\t  position zero, the local stream must retain its complete history — a\n    75\t  retention gap aborts publication before anything is emitted.\n    76\t- **The persisted node-blob format, both directions** — a\n    77\t  `dao.data.btree/IStorage` adapter over a DaoJing content-store handle\n    78\t  (`dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage`): nodes store as plain-EDN\n    79\t  content-addressed segment blobs (leaf `{:keys [...]}`, branch\n    80\t  `{:level n :keys [...] :addresses [...]}`), addresses minted by\n    81\t  `dao.jing/materialize!` — Merkle by construction, since `dao.data.btree`\n    82\t  stores children before parents. `read-manifest` validates a manifest and its\n    83\t  content address; `read-datoms` walks the EAVT node graph eagerly using only\n    84\t  `jing/get`; `restored-indexes` re-attaches a published manifest's trees\n    85\t  lazily on every platform.\n    86\t- **The transportable read coordinate** — `published-index` constructs a\n    87\t  serializable exact-bounded descriptor from a DaoJing coordinate and an\n    88\t  immutable manifest address. Opening it validates the descriptor, opens the\n    89\t  coordinate, fetches only the manifest, re-attaches the covered sets\n    90\t  lazily (restored-indexes), and returns a read-only closed realization whose\n    91\t  EAVT rows are deferred behind a delay. Physical B-tree nodes never cross\n    92\t  the stream boundary.\n    93\t- **The transactor entry point** — `publish-index!`: snapshot the stream,\n    94\t  build the four covered indexes, append the node blobs and the manifest to\n    95\t  one intake stream selected from an explicit pool. DaoJing itself is never\n    96\t  mutated directly.\n    97\t\n    98\t## The manifest\n    99\t\n   100\tThe published manifest is exactly\n   101\t\n   102\t```clojure\n   103\t{:indexes {:eavt <segment-address-or-nil>\n   104\t           :aevt <segment-address-or-nil>\n   105\t           :avet <segment-address-or-nil>\n   106\t           :vaet <segment-address-or-nil>}\n   107\t :count n\n   108\t :branching-factor n}\n   109\t```\n   110\t\n   180\t- **An empty stream publishes nil index addresses** —\n   181\t  `{:indexes {:eavt nil …}}` reads back as no datoms, not an error (a walk of\n   182\t  nil ⇒ ()).\n   183\t- **Publication changes representation, not visibility** — append has already\n   184\t  written the datoms to the local stream. Publication replaces no root; it\n   185\t  adds a persisted covered-index manifest over the same datoms.\n   186\t\n   187\t## The agent-transactor loop\n   188\t\n   189\tThe write path runs through `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\n   190\twrapper (see `dao.space.md`, *The Write Path*). Its descriptor is\n   191\t`{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s :intake-pool [...] optional :name}`; it\n   192\towns neither stream lifecycle — the local stream and intake pool are\n   193\tsupplied, never created, registered, or closed:\n   194\t\n   195\t```clojure\n   196\t(require '[dao.stream :as ds]\n   197\t         '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\n   198\t\n   199\t(def local (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer}))          ; the agent's own log\n   200\t(def intake-pool [(ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer})])  ; DaoJing intake streams\n   201\t\n   202\t(def log (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\n   203\t                    :local-stream local\n   204\t                    :intake-pool intake-pool\n   205\t                    :name \"worker-7\"}))             ; one wrapper per local stream\n   206\t\n   207\t(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/claims task})      ; 1. deposit — one atomic\n   208\t;; ... more appends ...                             ;    transaction record\n   209\t(transactor/publish! log)                           ; 2. snapshot, build, enqueue\n   210\t```\n   211\t\n   212\tThe wrapper's `ds/append!` / `transact!` write exactly one atomic transaction\n   213\trecord to the local stream per call, so no reader observes a torn transaction.\n   214\tOn open it scans the retained history from cursor zero and derives the next\n   215\t`t` (0 for an empty history, else 1 + the maximum datom t); full retention is\n   216\ttherefore currently required. One wrapper per local stream is a hard\n   217\tsingle-writer invariant. Calls through one wrapper serialize timestamp\n   218\tallocation and append on shared-memory hosts; two wrappers over the same\n   219\tstream still each derive the same `t` and write colliding records, which\n   220\tcannot be silently coordinated without shared mutable state. `publish!` delegates explicitly to\n   221\t`index/publish-index!`, passing the wrapper's local stream, intake pool, and\n   222\topts, and returning its `{:manifest-address ... :manifest ...}`.\n   223\t\n   224\tTwo lifecycle facts are deliberate:\n   225\t\n   226\t- Publication is an acceleration, never a semantic change — `q`/`match`\n   227\t  answer identically before and after (pinned by\n   228\t  `publish-index-snapshot-reads-local-stream-and-reads-back` and the\n   229\t  observer-materialization parity tests).\n   230\t- The published-index DaoStream adapter opens lazily (manifest fetch plus\n   231\t  restored sets) and defers its EAVT rows; query consumes the restored sets\n   232\t  directly for selective current reads, forces the rows for rest-pattern and\n   233\t  4+-slot scans, and walks the restored tree for unselective 3-fixed scans.\n   234\t  Query does not dispatch on manifests, pools, or B-tree segments. Publishing changes access cost, never\n   235\t  the datoms.\n   236\t\n   237\t## Dependency picture\n   238\t\n   239\t```\n   240\tdao.space.transactor  ──►  dao.space.index  ◄──  dao.space.query\n   250\t                                │\n   251\t                                ▼\n   252\t                          dao.jing (content-store handle)\n   253\t```\n   254\t\n   255\t- `dao.space.query` requires `dao.space.index` for manifest reading and index\n   256\t  traversal. It does not define or persist a second index format.\n   257\t- `dao.space.transactor` requires `dao.space.index` for snapshotting,\n   258\t  validation, and publication. The write path does not depend on the Datalog\n   259\t  engine.\n   260\t- `dao.space.index` requires `dao.data.btree`,\n   261\t  `dao.data.btree.storage`, and `dao.jing`; it never requires\n   262\t  `dao.space.query`. No cycle is possible: realization below, interpretation\n   263\t  above.\n   264\t\n   265\tStorage stays dumb throughout (Decision 1 of `dao.space.query.md`, no new\n   266\tstorage protocol): everything\n   267\there is built from `dao.jing/materialize!` and `dao.jing/get` against strict\n   268\tsegment addresses. Storage never knows the segment blobs form an index, and\n   269\tthere is no mutable root for it to maintain.\n   270\t\n   271\t## Platform status\n   272\t\n   273\tBuild, the eager published-stream adapter, lazy lower-level restoration, and\n   274\trange slicing are all\n   275\t**cross-platform**. `dao.data.btree` and `dao.data.btree.storage` are `.cljc`\n   276\timplementations shared by JVM, ClojureScript, and ClojureDart.\n   277\t\n   278\tThe published-stream path uses `walk-index-datoms`, which understands the plain EDN node\n   279\tblobs using only `jing/get`. The separate lazy API uses\n   280\t`dao.data.btree/restore-tree` through\n   281\t`dao.data.btree.storage/kv-storage`; traversal faults only the required nodes.\n   282\tThe manifest's `:count` and `:branching-factor` are threaded through\n   283\t`restore-tree` deliberately: count keeps O(1) `count` on restored trees\n   284\twithout faulting the graph, and the branching factor reaches every restored\n   285\tnode so mutation splits at the published thresholds. Tests in\n   286\t`test/dao/space/index_test.cljc` pins both contracts, including lazy\n   287\tpoint-lookup fetch counts for the lower-level restored tree and descriptor\n   288\ttransport/eager logical d5 reads for the DaoStream adapter.\n   289\t\n   290\t## Open items\n   291\t\n   292\t- **Segment GC** — superseded index segments accumulate forever.\n   293\t- **Published indexed snapshots and K-way merge** — queries currently consume\n   294\t  each published manifest through the eager logical stream adapter.\n   295\t- **Incremental indexing** — the natural next increment for long-lived agent\n   296\t  transactors: today an owner republishes wholesale from the full datom seq. A\n   297\t  future builder could retain the previous manifest, insert only the appended\n   298\t  datoms into restored B-trees, and store their changed paths. Nothing in the\n   299\t  node or manifest format requires that strategy; the current implementation\n   300\t  always performs a full rebuild.\n   301\t- **Async hydration** — remote reads over async backends use the hydration\n   302\t  adapter (`dao.data.btree.storage/hydration-storage`, `hydrate!`); the async\n   303\t  variants (`hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`) are deferred until an async\n   304\t  DaoJing backend exists.\n   235\t  so a schema entity in stream A and the \"same\" one in stream B are joined by\n   236\t  their `:db/ident` value — the stigmergy cross-stream identity ruling, with\n   237\t  no sixth tuple slot and no reserved entity block.\n   238\t- **No side channel.** The schema is not a second db-value carried next to\n   239\t  the data; it is read from the same source, and therefore discoverable by\n   240\t  `q` — the stigmergy \"vocabulary is the prompt\" convention becomes a\n   241\t  literal query an agent's runtime runs at startup.\n   242\t\n   243\t\n   244\t## 3. The write boundary: a validating wrapper\n   245\t\n   246\tDatomic's schema is enforced by its transactor. Here the schema interpreter\n   247\tis a library duty wrapping `dao.space.transactor`'s `:transactor` stream\n   248\twrapper — the same layering `yin.vm` already demonstrates with its own\n   249\tDatomic-style schema map and `datoms->tx-data`\n   250\t(`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `schema`): a client above the space declaring and\n   251\tenforcing a vocabulary at its own write boundary.\n   252\t\n   253\tThe wrapper validates **before emission** — the only sane order in an\n   254\tappend-only world, because there is no rollback after an append. A rejected\n   255\ttransaction throws with nothing appended; the stream stays clean.\n   355\t- **Schema rows themselves** — the wrapper validates `:db/*` rows at\n   356\t  transact: known property names only, legal `:db.type/*` and\n   357\t  `:db.cardinality/*` values, keyword `:db/ident`s, `:db/ident` uniqueness\n   358\t  on the vocabulary itself, and no re-declaration or retraction of a `:db/*`\n   359\t  attribute's own properties (§2 axioms). These are schema-structure\n   360\t  checks: both modes reject (§3's mode split), save the verbatim-axiom\n   361\t  carve-out that lets the bootstrap itself through.\n   362\t\n   363\t**Wrapper state, declared.** The wrapper maintains one per-wrapper\n   364\tcurrent-state index, built from a bounded snapshot after the inner transactor\n   365\tperforms its own cursor-zero watermark scan, and advanced per successful\n   366\tappend. A transaction containing schema rows takes another bounded snapshot\n   367\tto interpret its candidate next schema epoch. Data transactions advance the\n   368\tprojections incrementally; a schema epoch change rebuilds them against the\n   369\tnew declarations. The index serves\n   370\tthree duties at once: uniqueness, current values for supersession (§3.3), and\n   371\tlookup-ref resolution. It is per-wrapper and single-writer, the same duty\n   372\tclass as the `t` watermark, not new hidden mutability. The whole plan,\n   373\tvalidate, append, and state-advance transition is serialized through that one\n   374\twrapper, mirroring the inner transactor's single-writer boundary; no check can\n   375\trace another append through the same wrapper. **The schema is read\n   376\tfrom the stream at open, with `q`**: the wrapper drains its retained history\n   377\tinto a relation and runs the same extraction query as the §4 view — one\n   378\textraction function, two interpreters. The stream carries its own schema\n   379\t(§2). A schema transaction is validated as a proposed next epoch before\n   380\temission and becomes effective for the following transaction. Assertions and\n   381\tretractions both update the running wrapper's schema and derived indexes; no\n   382\treopen is required. Ownership is explicit: `schema/transactor`\n   383\topens the inner `:transactor` and owns it — its `close!` delegates inward,\n   384\tand the inner wrapper never leaks.\n   385\t\n   386\t### 3.2 The crucial non-check: cardinality\n   387\t\n   388\t**Cardinality-one is not a write constraint.** In an append-only world,\n   389\tcard-one is temporal supersession — a view rule, not a rejection. Two rows\n   390\t`[e a v1 t1]` and `[e a v2 t2]` with different values are both legal facts;\n   540\tpositional indexes* open item in `dao.space.query.md` lands: its planner rule\n   541\t(\"selects only from those supplied indexes and falls back to a relation scan\n   542\twhen none fits\") is the precondition that makes partial membership a cost\n   543\tdecision rather than an answer change.\n   544\t\n   545\tThere is no `:schema-address`: the schema lives in the source (§2), so\n   546\tthere is no standalone schema segment to content-address, and the\n   547\tprovenance of an interpretation is the source itself, optionally `as-of`'d.\n   548\tThe manifest is schema-independent — a complete covered set over the d5 log.\n   549\t\n   550\tThe published descriptor is its own type, realized by a registered\n   551\t`(ds/defopen :dao.space.schema/published …)` that delegates to the\n   552\t`published-index` realization — it cannot reuse that opener directly, since\n   553\t`open!` dispatches on `:dao.stream/type` and the index opener demands exact\n   554\tdescriptor equality. The shape carries what `validate-descriptor!` requires:\n   555\t\n   556\t```clojure\n   557\t{:dao.stream/type :dao.space.schema/published\n   558\t :dao.stream/bound {:manifest-address :segment/sha256-…}  ; the exact bound\n   559\t :dao.stream/comparator :dao.space.index/eavt\n   560\t :content-store <coordinate>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
